  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 AM CDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nThrough Tuesday: Heat is still the main story for today as \nmid/upper ridging builds across the region. High temperatures will\ncontinue to reach the upper 90s up to around 100 across the \nArkLaMiss Delta, with heat indices peaking in the 105-110F range \nareawide. Based on yesterday's observations, the heat advisory has\nbeen expanded to include the entire forecast area for today. An \nadvisory will likely be needed again for a portion of the area \ntomorrow, however, drier easterly flow will begin to arrive and \nthis may begin to limit heat stress for areas east of I-55. Given \nconsiderable deep layer moisture lingering across the area, \nisolated to scattered afternoon convection is expected again \ntoday. A few of these storms could again produce strong wind \ngusts. As drier air begins to arrive tomorrow, coverage is \nexpected to decrease. \n\nWednesday through next Monday: Continuing easterly flow will \nusher in a relatively drier airmass through the end of the work \nweek. This airmass will still support hot days, with temps still \napproaching the triple digits in the ArkLaMiss Delta. But with \ndewpoints dropping into 60s and perhaps the 50s, heat stress will \nbecome less of a concern through the end of the week. It is still \npossible a heat advisory may be needed for some areas mainly west \nof I-55 on Wednesday, but heat related alerts are unlikely for the\nremainder of the week. While days will still be quite warm with \nabove normal temps, mornings will see temps dipping into the 60s \nin many areas. Deep layer moisture will begin to increase again \nthis weekend, with returning potential for diurnal convection each\nday. Though it will still be warm, high temps are expected to \nregress closer to seasonal normals around the beginning of next \nweek. /DL/",
